 "B_packet_quality_note": "All B_runs below use sigma = 2.5. At k0 = 1.5 the packet's momentum spread 1/(2 sigma k0) = 0.13 and the two wall estimators agree to 0.8% (dx1/T = -1.006, dx2/T = -0.999). At k0 = 0.8 that ratio is 0.25, which the frozen spin-1/2 protocol already warns is too large (the slow small-k components never leave the wall); those rows read dx1/T = -1.099 / dx2/T = -0.927, the two estimators BRACKETING -1 with a 0.17 spread. This is a packet-quality artifact, not a spin-1 effect: the spin-1/2 CONTROL rows at the same k0 = 0.8 show the identical pattern (dx1/T = -2.231, dx2/T = -1.868, i.e. the same +11%/-7% bracket about -2). A wider-packet re-run (sigma = 5, standoff = 26, N = 120) was launched to tighten these four rows and was stopped before completing; it can only tighten the k0 = 0.8 error bars, since both estimators already bracket the predicted -1/s and the k0 = 1.5 rows, the N-convergence (N = 72/80/88, identical to 5 decimals), the dt-convergence and the D-truncation rows are all inside the 8% window."
